DTrace review: Fuchsia, CADETS and HyperTrace (2026-10-13)
========================================================

This is a source review and proposal, not an implementation or performance
claim. The request to avoid system modification is applied to tracing-engine
changes and live deployment. Separately authorized USDT additions remain in
the twentieth pass. No tracing infrastructure from another OS was imported.

Existing facilities in this checkout
-----------------------------------
DTrace already provides dynamic instrumentation, predicates, in-kernel
aggregation, speculation, per-CPU buffering and selectable buffer policy.
Do not sell rings, filtering or histograms as missing features. Inspect
cddl/contrib/opensolaris/lib/libdtrace/common/dt_options.c and the kernel
DTrace implementation for policy and drop semantics.

CADETS-derived work is partly present: share/man/man4/dtrace_audit.4 and the
audit provider, safety tests for jail identity, and libxo-backed libdtrace
printing. dtrace.c calls xo_parse_args and dtrace_oformat_configure; dt_print.c,
dt_handle.c and dt_aggregate.c contain structured-output paths. A targeted
correctness audit is justified: libxo_specified in main is declared without
an initializer, conditionally assigned, and subsequently read. This review
records it without changing the engine/CLI.

bsdinstruments already has profiles, filters, JSONL, OTLP and collapsed-stack
export; libotelexport already reports DTrace drops to OTLP. libtracecmp and
BSDTrace already support authorized descriptor delegation and buffer sizing.
They do not implement D-language policy isolation for untrusted scripts.
The oe_event representation currently carries wall time and a rendered body;
there are no typed duration/flow fields in that structure. Drop metadata is
currently documented as a no-op for non-OTLP exporters. These are concrete
integration gaps, not reasons to replace DTrace.

Fuchsia findings and proposed adoption
-------------------------------------
Fuchsia has explicit duration, counter, asynchronous and flow events with
correlation IDs. Its workflow opens traces in Perfetto. Adopt this vocabulary
in versioned userspace trace metadata and export it through bsdinstruments.
Start with channel/RPC handoffs and bhyve save/restore phases. Request IDs must
be scoped by boot/session, process instance and VM instance; raw pointers,
reused PIDs and security tokens are unsuitable persistent identities. Cross-
process causal edges need an actual propagated or observed correlation key;
matching two timestamps is not proof of a causal relationship.
Sources:
https://fuchsia.dev/fuchsia-src/development/tracing/trace_events
https://fuchsia.dev/fuchsia-src/development/tools/ffx/workflows/record-traces

Fuchsia providers write a binary record stream into shared VMOs. The format
uses compact string/thread references and permits recovery through the last
valid record. Borrow compact, versioned capture archives with explicit record
lengths and metadata retention; add Perfetto export as a consumer feature.
A shared-memory fast path would be a distinct USDT transport design requiring
measurement and process-lifetime safeguards, not a drop-in DTrace optimization.
Source: https://fuchsia.dev/fuchsia-src/reference/tracing/trace-format

Fuchsia supports oneshot, circular and streaming modes; streaming can drop
records when draining falls behind. It does not promise lossless tracing.
DTrace already has related buffer policies. Improve consistent export of
loss intervals, overflow reasons, clock domains and capture configuration.
Invalidate affected latency/causal results rather than quietly presenting
incomplete captures as complete. Include monotonic event time and explicit
wall-clock conversion anchors, rather than treating collector time as firing
time. Provider restart and VM restore need new clock/lifetime epochs.
Source: https://fuchsia.dev/fuchsia-src/concepts/kernel/tracing-provider-buffering-modes

CADETS findings and proposed adoption
------------------------------------
CADETS emphasizes causal provenance and adaptive distributed observation.
Its published FreeBSD work describes structured output and audit integration,
which are already partly represented here. The useful next step is a small
causal schema linking resource creation, ownership transfer and release, with
coverage and loss annotations. Use low-volume aggregates to trigger bounded
higher-detail captures. Avoid copying every syscall payload or claiming that
DTrace is a tamper-proof, lossless security audit trail. Existing audit policy
must continue to govern that use case.
Sources:
https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/research/security/cadets/
https://freebsdfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/Cadets-Blending-Tracing-and-Security-on-FreeBSD.pdf

HyperTrace findings and proposed adoption
----------------------------------------
The relevant project is cadets/freebsd, not the unrelated Hypertrace cloud
observability product. Its README describes a target field in probe selectors,
ELF-encoded programs, DIF typing/relocations, dtraced program distribution,
tagged guest addresses, and self-instrumenting guests calling the host through
hypercalls. It also lists virtio transport and bhyve integration. The inspected
/usr/src lacks those distinctive DTrace modules, compiler files and selector
support. Prior local ownership or another branch is not evidence of integration
in this checkout. No exhaustive audit of every external checkout was performed.

Adopt explicit VM identity and host/guest capture coordination first; isolate
per-VM maps and resource quotas, validate guest types and addresses, and revoke
sessions on reset. A full HyperTrace port is a separate compiler/runtime/VMM
project. The README notes missing retransmission after guest death; restore,
migration, stale probe maps and tracing-session revocation need explicit tests.
Synchronous hypercalls give host-side observation order but incur VM exits;
they do not prove causal order or free high-rate tracing. Benchmark selective
hypercalls against batched guest export before choosing a default transport.
Source: https://github.com/cadets/freebsd
Background: https://freebsdfoundation.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/918ab1cb58d4efe919ab11c7648eb92e35c343ba.1.pdf

Recommended sequence and acceptance criteria (our engineering judgment)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
P0: Qualify installed probe availability and enabled/disabled correctness.
    Use the new DOF checks plus live firing in a qualification VM. Add a
    structured-output regression suite and fix the observed initialization bug
    in a separately reviewed DTrace change.
P0: Versioned event schema, monotonic clock provenance and loss metadata across
    every exporter. Typed duration/flow plus Perfetto export can start in
    userspace without replacing the kernel engine.
P1: Adaptive capture profiles and bounded budgets in the existing trace broker.
    A raw delegated DTrace descriptor is not a sandbox for untrusted D code.
P1: Host/guest identity, coordinated collection and bhyve restore epochs;
    prototype selected HyperTrace capabilities behind opt-in configuration.
P2: Consider shared-memory USDT transport or DIF specialization/JIT only after
    profiling identifies trap/VM-exit cost or interpreter cost as the bottleneck.
    New executable code paths need their own safety and architecture validation.

Benchmark disabled, enabled-empty, predicate-rejected, aggregated and raw-export
cases separately. Include Zsh pipelines, Capsule RPC, channel saturation,
network throughput, storage latency and guest I/O. Record cycles/event, p50/p99
application latency, throughput, CPU, memory, allocation rates, drops and export
backpressure; sweep CPU count, event rate, argument size and stack depth. Measure
both no-probe baseline and compiled-in-but-disabled probes. Publish variance
and build/hardware configuration. No numerical speedup is established here.
